   check: `E0203 impl of trait 'IntoIterator' is missing associated type 'IntoIter'`.

2. With `type IntoIter = VecIntoIter[i64];` added (accepted), a generic consumer still cannot reach the method, because the trait declares none:

    fn total[C: IntoIterator[Item = i64]](c: own C) -> i64 {
        let mut s = 0;
        for x in c.into_iter() { s += x; }
        s
    }

   check: `no method 'into_iter' on type parameter 'C'; add a trait bound declaring it`. This is the generic-library shape (a function taking any consumable collection) that docs/library uses for `Vec.extend`, `collect` sources and friends.

3. Still with the `IntoIter` line, `let mut v: Vec[i64] = Vec.new(); v.extend(Bag { items: vec![3, 4] });` passes `karac check` (B-2026-10-13-19 special-cased `extend` in src/typechecker/method_vec_mutation.rs rather than going through the trait), and `__mir-run` then refuses it: `error[mir]: build: 2286:25 in vec_extend_iter[i64, Bag]: the method call .next with no resolved callee is not lowered yet`. So check passes a program nothing runs. A direct `b.into_iter().count()` on the same Bag runs (prints 2): the impl's method is reachable by name on the concrete type, just not through the trait.

SOURCE NOTE: found while probing kata 811 (Subdomain Visit Count); its iterator arm's `flat_map` closure returns a `Vec`, which needs `.into_iter()` (flat_map's `U: Iterator` is the spec's, docs/library/iterators.md), and the probe went on to the trait itself. The kata needed no workaround: `Vec.into_iter()` is inherent. Owner: Thread A (stdlib in Kāra: iterators).

Measured on main 477e314d59 (release `karac`), `karac check` and `karac __mir-run`:

    fn main() {
        let n = 4;
        let pairs: Vec[i64] = (0..n).flat_map(|i| (i + 1..n).map(|j| i * 10 + j)).collect();
        println(f"{pairs}");
    }

check: `E0509 the result borrows i, which this function owns, so it would outlive it (core-semantics.md §5.4)` at the inner range. __mir-run: `borrow check main.c0: bb4[term]: the result borrows _2, which this function owns [E0509]`. With the captured value bound to a local first, `flat_map(|i| { let base = i * 10; (i + 1..n).map(|j| base + j) })`, both report E0516 (`cannot end base while it is still borrowed`) and the MIR one adds E0509. Expected `[1, 2, 3, 12, 13, 23]`.

The spec: §9.1 says a capture whose place dies while the closure is still live is by move instead, whatever the type, and a `Copy` place is copied; `i` (and `base`) die when the outer closure's call returns, while the inner closure lives on inside the returned `IterMap`. §9.3 independently makes a closure inside a returned value escaping, which captures every place by move. Rust's spelling is `move |j|`; design.md's table of rejected keywords sends `move` to E_MOVE_NOT_USED, so a Kāra user has no way to ask for the copy and the canonical nested `flat_map` for pairs and triples cannot be written.

