SOURCE NOTE: found writing kata 946 (Validate Stack Sequences), whose `pattern` arm builds `p.push(place.get(*v).unwrap())` with `place: Map[i64, i64]` and `p: Vec[i64]`; measured 2026-10-24 with the release karac at 9913c68556 and runtime archives at 5502146bed. Repro:

    fn main() {
        let mut place: Map[i64, i64] = Map.new();
        place.insert(5, 1);
        place.insert(6, 0);
        let mut p: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
        p.push(place.get(5).unwrap());
        p.push(place.get(6).unwrap());
        println(f"{p[0]} {p[1]}");
        if p[1] < p[0] { println("less"); } else { println("not less"); }
    }

Expected `1 0` then `less` (legacy `karac run --interp` prints that). `karac check`: All checks passed. `karac __mir-llvm-run`: `94847549067688 94847549067704` then `not less`, the two elements being consecutive heap addresses 16 apart and compared as such. `karac __mir-run`: prints `1 0`, then `error[mir]: run: in main at bb16[0]: Lt on Ref(Addr { root: Heap(AllocId(1)), path: [1, 1] }) and Ref(Addr { ... path: [0, 1] })`, exit 3. `karac run` (default) prints `not less` like mir-llvm.

What changes it (each a separate probe, same values): binding the unwrap first, `let x = place.get(5).unwrap(); p.push(x);`, gives `1 0 / less` on both backends, as do `let x: i64 = ...`, `p.push(*place.get(5).unwrap())` (also accepted by check), `p.push(a[0])` and `for v in a.iter() { p.push(v); }`. A user function taking `i64`, `twice(place.get(5).unwrap())`, gets the value (42 from 21). `Vec.get` has the same fault: `p.push(a.get(1).unwrap())` with `a = vec![4, 9]` prints `9` under `__mir-run` but `94533187680440` under mir-llvm and a `karac build --mir` binary; `p.push(place.get(5).unwrap() + 0)` prints 21 everywhere. So the narrow shape is an `Option[ref T].unwrap()` call written directly as the argument of `Vec.push` on a `Vec[T]` of a Copy `T`: check accepts the `ref i64` where `i64` is expected (an implicit copy), and the MIR builder stores the reference itself, with no load, into the element. Inferred, not traced: the push lowering takes the argument operand as is when its type is a ref to the element type.

The kata routes nothing around it: the `pattern` arm keeps the natural spelling and its output happened to match under mir-llvm (the address order of a Map's values follows the push places on that input), while `__mir-run` refuses it, so kata 946 is held until this is fixed or the arm is reworded on a spec decision.

SOURCE NOTE: found by Thread B while pinning the drop schedule's requested case for B-2026-10-21-59 (an adapter that holds a `mut ref` to a struct owning a Vec). The bug is already on origin/main at 4540c92380, so B-2026-10-21-59's change did not cause it. Program:

struct Own { v: Vec[i64] }
struct Lend { o: mut ref Own }
impl Own {
    fn lend(mut ref self) -> Lend { Lend { o: self } }
}
impl Lend {
    fn first(ref self) -> ref i64 { self.o.v.get(0).unwrap() }
}
fn main() {
    let mut o = Own { v: vec![1, 2, 3] };
    let l = o.lend();
    let x = l.first();
    o.v.push(4);
    println(f"{*x}");
}

Measured at 4540c92380: `karac check` reports `All checks passed.`, and `__mir-run` prints 1. On the B-2026-10-21-59 tree, `__mir-llvm-asan` reports `heap-use-after-free ... READ of size 8`, because the push reallocates the Vec's 3-element buffer. `l = o.lend()` borrows `o` (a `Lend` result keeps the receiver's place). `x = l.first()` borrows `l`. But after the call, `l` is never used again, so the loan on `o` that `l`'s value holds dies there, even though `x`, which borrows `l`, is still live. The fix probably has to keep a loan live while any loan on the value that holds it is live (a reborrow through a holder), or make `first`'s result borrow what `l` holds, the way `ResultBorrows::ReceiverValue` does for views. A lending `Iterator` written the same way (`next -> Option[ref i64]` reading `self.o.v`) is accepted for the same reason.
